# {{ project_name }}

{{ description }}. Built on the Brevia library from its project template.

## Structure

```text
brevia.toml                       project file read by Brevia: models, limits, activity defaults
prompts/                          prompt templates of the activities
src/{{ package_name }}/
  application.py                  creates Brevia and registers the activities
  activities/                     the code that runs inside a run or a job and uses Brevia
  domain/                         the logic of the application, without Brevia
  schemas.py                      requests, configuration and results
  api.py                          the HTTP API: the endpoints of Brevia and of the application
client.py                         a caller in Python: runs an activity, submits a job, reads its status
tests/                            tests with a fake model
compose.yaml                      development database; the application in a container (profile "app")
Dockerfile, bin/start             the image of the application and the script that starts it
```

## Setup

```sh
cp .env.example .env              # then set the token secret and the provider key
docker compose up -d --wait       # development database
uv sync
uv run brevia db upgrade          # Brevia schemas; again after every upgrade of Brevia
```

## Run

```sh
echo "A text to summarise." > text.txt
uv run --env-file .env python client.py run text.txt
```

As a job: a worker in one terminal, the submission in another.

```sh
uv run --env-file .env brevia worker {{ package_name }}.application:create
uv run --env-file .env python client.py submit summary-1 text.txt
```

Over HTTP: the API in one terminal, a worker in another, then a request with a token.

```sh
uv run --env-file .env uvicorn {{ package_name }}.api:app --reload
TOKEN=$(uv run --env-file .env brevia token create --user my-frontend)
curl -X POST localhost:8000/summaries -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
     -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"text": "A text to summarise."}'
RUN_ID=...                        # the run_id in the answer of POST /summaries
curl localhost:8000/runs/$RUN_ID -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"          # status: queued, running, succeeded
curl localhost:8000/runs/$RUN_ID/result -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"   # 409 not_ready until it succeeds
```

The API does not start without `BREVIA_TOKENS_SECRET` in `.env` (at least 32 bytes).

The interactive documentation of every endpoint is at http://localhost:8000/docs.

`BREVIA_FAKE_MODELS=true` in `.env` answers every model call with a fake: no provider key and
no cost, for local runs.

## Container

The image runs the worker and the API with `bin/start`, which `APP_MODE` drives:

| `APP_MODE` | Runs |
|---|---|
| `all` (default) | the worker and the API together; a stop reaches both and waits for them, and when one ends on its own the other is stopped and the container exits with an error |
| `api` | the API only, on `PORT` (8000) |
| `worker` | the worker only |
| `migrate` | `brevia db upgrade`, then exits: run it once per release, before the processes |

```sh
docker build -t {{ project_slug }} .                     # the image, for a platform

# Locally, with the database of compose.yaml: build first, so the migration runs the new code.
docker compose --profile app build
docker compose --profile app run --rm -e APP_MODE=migrate app
docker compose --profile app up                           # then http://localhost:8000/docs
```

`bin/start` needs bash 5.1 or later (the image has it) and the profile `app` needs
Docker Compose 2.24 or later. The build installs the packages of `uv.lock`, written by
`uv sync`: Brevia must come from a package index or a Git URL the build can reach, not
from a `file://` path. Keep the version of uv in the `Dockerfile` (0.13.0) as the one
that writes `uv.lock`.

The environment of the container needs at least:

| Variable | Why |
|---|---|
| `BREVIA_DATABASE_URL` | the database |
| `BREVIA_TOKENS_SECRET` | the tokens of the HTTP API, at least 32 bytes |
| `OPENAI_API_KEY` | the provider of the models |
| `BREVIA_FILES_ROOT` | a folder on a volume, for the files of runs |
| `BREVIA_APP_VERSION` | the release of the image: jobs interrupted under another release are not started again |
| `BREVIA_WORKER_ID` | a fixed name for the worker of this service, never shared by two running workers: the host name of a container changes at every deploy |

Set the stop timeout of the platform to `BREVIA_WORKER_SHUTDOWN_SECONDS` (30) plus a few
seconds, or running jobs are interrupted and start again from the beginning; some
platforms stop at once by default. `GET /health/ready` checks the database, not the
worker. The image runs as root, so it can write to volumes that platforms mount as root.
More in the guide *Running in production* of Brevia.

## Test

```sh
uv run pytest
```

The tests use a fake model; those that run an activity need the database and are skipped
without it.

## Template updates

The project records the template it comes from in `.copier-answers.yml`. To take the changes
of a newer template:

```sh
uvx copier update
```

## Document summary from the library

The application also registers `document.summarize` with the library's readers,
packaged prompts and `SummarizeConfig`. Use `POST /runs` with
`{"activity":"document.summarize","mode":"job","input":{"text":"A document."}}`,
or pass a PDF, DOCX or UTF-8 text file by URL:
`"input":{"file":{"name":"report.pdf","url":"https://docs.example.org/report.pdf"}}`.
Files need `BREVIA_FILES_ROOT` and the host of the URL in `BREVIA_FILES_ALLOWED_HOSTS`
(see `.env.example`); without them the run fails with `unavailable` or `invalid_input`.
Its defaults are in the `activities."document.summarize"` section of `brevia.toml`.
The original `text.summarize` remains the example of domain checks written by the
project; `/summaries` submits that custom activity.
